<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalākaua</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as David Kalākaua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kalākaua was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> from 1874 to 1891. He secured the Reciprocity Treaty of 1875, which let Hawaiian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> duty-free, became the first reigning monarch to travel around the world in 1881 and revived Hawaiian traditions such as the hula. In 1887 planters forced him to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet-constitution">Bayonet Constitution</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> and was succeeded by his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalakaua.jpg" alt="Kalākaua" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James J. Williams · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdavidkalakaua_dust.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A small army and royal guard remained, and <b>Kalākaua</b>’s only warship, the Kaimiloa, sailed to Samoa in 1887, but the Honolulu Rifles, militia of the Hawaiian League, imposed the constitution of 1887, and in January 1893 Queen Liliʻuokalani yielded rather than fight the 162 American sailors and marines…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hawaiʻi State Legislature considered it for the official state song in 1967, and the Hawaiʻi State Constitutional Convention again in 1978, but both times &quot;Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī&quot; was chosen instead, a song written by King David <b>Kalākaua</b> and composed by Henri Berger, master of the Royal Hawaiian Band.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hawaii-aloha">Hawaiʻi Aloha</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī&quot; (&quot;Hawaii's Own&quot;) was written in 1874 by King David <b>Kalākaua</b> of the Hawaiian Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hawaii-ponoi">Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1889 King <b>Kalākaua</b> printed the chant as a sixty-page pamphlet, with a two-page paper on how it had been composed and recited.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kumulipo">Kumulipo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.31,-157.86&amp;year=1871" aria-label="Honolulu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="309 1376 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kalākaua"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M759 1646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.31,-157.86&amp;year=1871">What was here in 1871? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
